    <h3>Phase I: Dot-Com Bust and Early-2000s Chop (2000–2002)</h3>
    <p>Nike opened the millennium at ${first_close:.2f} and quickly fell into a severe bear market alongside the broader tech collapse. From a January 2000 peak of $4.91, the stock plunged <strong>49.7%</strong> to an all-time low of $2.47 by February 25, 2000. It took until December 26, 2000 — roughly 10 months — to reclaim the pre-drawdown high. The 2001–2002 period added two more major drawdowns of <strong>39.0%</strong> and <strong>38.4%</strong>, keeping the stock range-bound for nearly three years.</p>

    <h3>Phase II: The Great Compounding (2003–2007)</h3>
    <p>A powerful bull run began in early 2003. From roughly $4.36 at the start of 2003, Nike climbed to $12.60 by the end of 2007 — a <strong>four-year CAGR near 30%</strong>. This era was marked by consistent outperformance, minimal deep drawdowns, and a steady upward slope in the 200-day moving average.</p>

    <h3>Phase III: Global Financial Crisis (2008–2009)</h3>
    <p>The GFC delivered Nike’s third-largest peak-to-trough decline. From a June 2008 high of $13.84, the stock collapsed <strong>44.2%</strong> to $7.73 by March 9, 2009. Recovery was slow but steady; the pre-crisis high was not fully reclaimed until March 1, 2010 — nearly 21 months after the trough.</p>

    <h3>Phase IV: The Golden Decade (2010–2019)</h3>
    <p>The 2010s were Nike’s most rewarding period for long-term holders. The stock rose from $13.27 at the start of 2010 to $93.45 by the end of 2019 — a <strong>CAGR of 21.6%</strong>. Key drivers included digital transformation, international expansion, and margin expansion. Even during this bull market, Nike was not immune to corrections: a <strong>25.3%</strong> drawdown from late 2015 to late 2016 tested patience, requiring nearly 14 months to recover.</p>

    <h3>Phase V: Pandemic Shock and Parabolic Peak (2020–2021)</h3>
    <p>COVID-19 triggered a <strong>39.8%</strong> crash in just two months (January–March 2020), but Nike recovered with unprecedented speed — reclaiming its pre-pandemic high by June 3, 2020, only 72 days after the March 23 trough. The subsequent stimulus-driven rally pushed the stock to its all-time high of <strong>${ath_price:.2f}</strong> on {ath_date}.</p>

    <h3>Phase VI: The Protracted Bear Market (2022–2026)</h3>
    <p>Since the November 2021 peak, Nike has entered its longest and deepest drawdown on record. The stock fell <strong>68.5%</strong> to $52.31 by April 8, 2025, and as of January 30, 2026, remains <strong>{(1-last_close/ath_price)*100:.1f}%</strong> below the all-time high with no recovery in sight. This phase has erased the majority of gains accrued since 2019 and represents a fundamental repricing of the company’s growth outlook.</p>

